## Solution

We expand $f(x) = x$ on $(0, 3)$ in a Fourier–Bessel series of order $n = 1$:

$$f(x) = \sum_{m=1}^{\infty} c_m\, J_1(\alpha_m x), \qquad 0 < x < 3,$$

where $\alpha_m$ are the positive roots of the boundary condition $J_1(3\alpha) = 0$ (equivalently $3\alpha_m = j_{1,m}$, the $m$-th positive zero of $J_1$).

### Orthogonality and coefficient formula

The Bessel equation $(xy')' + (\alpha^2 x - 1/x)\,y = 0$ on $(0,3)$ with $y(3)=0$ and $y$ bounded at $0$ is a Sturm–Liouville problem with weight $w(x) = x$. The eigenfunctions $J_1(\alpha_m x)$ satisfy

$$\int_0^3 x\, J_1(\alpha_m x)\, J_1(\alpha_k x)\, dx = \frac{9}{2}\bigl[J_1'(3\alpha_m)\bigr]^2 \delta_{mk}.$$

Using $J_1'(z) = -J_2(z) + \tfrac{1}{z}J_1(z)$ and the boundary condition $J_1(3\alpha_m) = 0$, we get $J_1'(3\alpha_m) = -J_2(3\alpha_m)$, so the normalization is

$$N_m = \frac{9}{2}\, J_2(3\alpha_m)^2.$$

Hence

$$c_m = \frac{1}{N_m}\int_0^3 x\, f(x)\, J_1(\alpha_m x)\, dx = \frac{2}{9\, J_2(3\alpha_m)^2}\int_0^3 x^2\, J_1(\alpha_m x)\, dx.$$

### Evaluating the integral

Substitute $t = \alpha_m x$ ($dx = dt/\alpha_m$, limits $0$ to $3\alpha_m$):

$$\int_0^3 x^2\, J_1(\alpha_m x)\, dx = \frac{1}{\alpha_m^3}\int_0^{3\alpha_m} t^2\, J_1(t)\, dt.$$

Using the standard identity $\dfrac{d}{dt}\bigl[t^{\nu} J_{\nu}(t)\bigr] = t^{\nu} J_{\nu-1}(t)$ with $\nu = 2$:

$$\frac{d}{dt}\bigl[t^2 J_2(t)\bigr] = t^2 J_1(t).$$

Therefore

$$\int_0^{3\alpha_m} t^2\, J_1(t)\, dt = \Bigl[t^2 J_2(t)\Bigr]_0^{3\alpha_m} = (3\alpha_m)^2\, J_2(3\alpha_m) - 0 = 9\alpha_m^2\, J_2(3\alpha_m),$$

since $t^2 J_2(t) \to 0$ as $t \to 0$ (because $J_2(0) = 0$). Thus

$$\int_0^3 x^2\, J_1(\alpha_m x)\, dx = \frac{1}{\alpha_m^3}\cdot 9\alpha_m^2\, J_2(3\alpha_m) = \frac{9}{\alpha_m}\, J_2(3\alpha_m).$$

### The coefficients

$$c_m = \frac{2}{9\, J_2(3\alpha_m)^2}\cdot \frac{9}{\alpha_m}\, J_2(3\alpha_m) = \frac{2}{\alpha_m\, J_2(3\alpha_m)}.$$

### Result

$$\boxed{\,x = \sum_{m=1}^{\infty} \frac{2}{\alpha_m\, J_2(3\alpha_m)}\, J_1(\alpha_m x), \qquad 0 < x < 3,\,}$$

where $\alpha_m$ ($m = 1, 2, 3, \dots$) are the positive roots of $J_1(3\alpha) = 0$, i.e. $\alpha_m = j_{1,m}/3$ with $j_{1,m}$ the $m$-th positive zero of $J_1$.

